              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_references"} Related references
          %h2#preliminaries Preliminaries
          %p
            The non-toy example application that gives the paper its title is to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
          %p
            The notion of quantization discussed builds on the notion of
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
            %em
              families of
              = succeed "-groupoids" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            that appears in some of the later sections of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+the+Classification+of+Topological+Field+Theories"} On the Classification of Topological Field Theories
              \.
          %p
            Together with a notion of
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"} vertical categorification
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-vector+space"} 2-vector space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            for “finite” theories such as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dijkgraaf-Witten+theory"} Dijkgraaf-Witten theory
          %p
            The development, sketching a rather grand picture, remains somewhat sketchy, though, possibly due to the fact that this is a conference proceedings. Also some of the ideas claimed to now be fully generalized have appeared elsewhere before. Notably the notion of the quantization map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Fam
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fam_n(C) \to C
            (see below) is effectively what
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"} John Baez
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Dolan"} Jim Dolan
            call in their program on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoidification"} groupoidification
            call
            %em degroupoidification
            \. The general idea underlying this, that spaces of states are computed as colimits of sections, has been made clear previously by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Willerton"} Simon Willerton
            %em The twisted Drinfeld double of a finite group via gerbes and finite groupoids
            (
            %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.QA/0503266"}> arXiv:math/0503266
            )
          %h2#the_general_abstract_notion_of_quantization_for_discrete_theories The general abstract notion of quantization for discrete theories
          %blockquote
            %p
              Here is a summary of the general quantization aspect of the article, together with some additional remarks on how to think of all this by
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} an nLab author
          %p
            The following is the formalization of the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            for discrete theories (such as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dijkgraaf-Witten+theory"} Dijkgraaf-Witten theory
          %p
            Fix some
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            to be described.
          %p In
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+the+Classification+of+Topological+Field+Theories"} On the Classification of Topological Field Theories
              \.
          %p
            are described the following two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category"} (∞,n)-categories
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category+of+cobordisms"} (∞,n)-category of cobordisms
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
